r but  for me facilitated the single greatest  ending the series has ever seen  and the best part about it is that it  utilized and made necessary the rules of  the game  in order to fend off these bombers to  buy time for golden kilowatt to heal and  prepare the rules of the game and the  mechanics of it are used to full effect  and in interesting strategic ways  as they become the focus of the  narrative for some time  counting how many accompany cards they  have as well as stalking out places to  replenish the supply  this very much feels like a tense and  realistic depiction of the struggles one  might find in the real world if they  were to play this game what's more is  when the action does  reach fever pitch and gone does finally  go head to head against the bomber the  decisions made visually with the  implementation of gore makes it  immediately memorable while also  reinforcing the psychotic tendencies  gon possesses and how he's developed a  pride in himself over the course of this  story i'm not sure i'm a fan of the  latter from a moral standpoint but  narratively it's really solid  considering the way gone is developing  once again the way gone utilizes the  rules and the cards at his disposal  along with some clever planning and  thinking with his nan yielded one of the  best hand to hand or hand to stub  contest that this story has thrown my  way yet  with even the serial killer bomber guy  remarking at how crazy gone seems to be  acting and  he is of the three battles that  transpire here gonz  is the most significant from a plot  standpoint but that's not to say that  the others don't have something to offer  too  killer's battle is standard kilowatt  affair cam cool  collected and with a ton of great action  paneling biscuit  on the other hand had something else to  offer  and that's when the prince realized that  when you grab a bull  by the horns sometimes  he'll take you for a ride  now this is cool and all but it makes me  wonder if there are 